# sixteen doubles are live across the join behind two ways that call, and the third way returns at once: the two ways are framed parts, and so is what stands behind the join, whose head is the join.
# x86-64 has sixteen registers for doubles, one of them the scratch register: one parameter of the join gets none. It is spilled, with no register at its definition, so the edges into the join store it.
# those edges run where the frame of the head is not set up: the ways in front tear theirs down behind the edge moves. The word lay below the stack pointer, and the call behind the join wrote over it: 345.5 in the place of 345.75.
# where a head has such a parameter the frame is set up at the entry after all
# f1 = g, f2 = h, f3 = last, f4 = looped, f5 = straight
